      "locale": "en",
      "text": "it approaches. The pace is steadier than the headlines suggest. Most months hold a full moon, a new moon, and a station or two; eclipses come roughly twice a year, in pairs; the slow planets change signs a few times a decade. When a date here feels heavy, the Almanac usually has the longer story of its month. Behind this calendar Coverage, sources, and method Events cover 2026 through the end of 2027, drawn from the site's committed sky catalogs — the same astronomy behind the Almanac , the retrograde tables , and the eclipse calendar . Instants are universal time, exact as printed. Lunations and eclipses list the Moon's sign and degree at the listed instant; the calendar shows slow-planet sign changes and slow-pair exact aspects, while the faster daily traffic lives in the horoscopes and the monthly Almanac. How the site computes and reads the sky covers both halves of the method."
