      "locale": "en",
      "text": "not let it ask for the attention it needs. It can perform fine for a long time — chin up, lights on — while waiting for someone to notice the act. The kindest thing a partner can learn is to notice early. The growth edge The work is separating applause from love. Applause responds to the shine; love stays for the unlit hours, and a Leo Moon that has only ever offered its polished self may quietly believe the unpolished one is unlovable. It isn’t, but the placement has to test that the hard way: by being seen tired, unfunny, and unimpressive, and finding that the room stays warm. The mature version also learns to ask plainly. Radiating and hoping is a strategy; a sentence is a tool. I need some of your attention tonight costs one moment of pride and saves whole seasons of silent arithmetic. When that lands, this Moon becomes what it always suspected it was: not the person who needs"
