      "text": "thirteen years of births raised with every worldview on earth visible at once, each in earshot of the others. The generational tell is fluency in other people’s certainties. These are people with an ear for how belief sounds from the inside, a passport-stamp approach to ideas, and a reflexive suspicion of anyone claiming the view from nowhere. Belief under pressure Truth used to arrive with credentials — the pulpit, the lectern, the byline — and power sat with whoever issued them. This cohort watched certainty get loud and trust get quiet, which is a Pluto curriculum in itself. Their long renovation is the question of how conviction gets earned: not whether meaning exists, but what a belief has to survive before it deserves the name. The"
