      "heading": "Leo and Virgo",
      "locale": "en",
      "text": "stage. Leo courts back at premiere volume, and Virgo — protesting mildly, privately delighted — discovers what it’s like to be somebody’s occasion instead of somebody’s staff. Where it grinds The note. Virgo offers a correction — small, accurate, intended as devotion — and Leo receives a review of the entire crown. The recurring fight starts here every single time: Leo hears criticism as adoration being withdrawn; Virgo hears Leo’s wounded reaction as proof the help isn’t wanted. Virgo then goes quiet and keeps fixing things, unthanked. Leo performs harder and feels unseen. Both end up giving at full capacity in a language the other doesn’t count as giving. The second grind is applause. Virgo will not gush on cue — its compliments are rationed, precise, and earned — and Leo cannot live on a “fine.”"
