      "heading": "Born on April 2",
      "locale": "en",
      "text": "anniversaries kept, partners praised in company, effort made visible. It bruises when affection has to be inferred. At work the same wiring produces a morale engine — the colleague whose confidence is contagious, who takes the blame in the room and the argument to management. Best conditions: real stakes, clear ownership, an audience of at least one. The applause problem The friction is that the performance needs a house. When nobody’s watching — the unglamorous stretch of a project, the caretaking nobody counts — motivation can sag, and April 2 starts hunting for a stage instead of finishing the scene. The steadying practice is keeping one commitment that stays entirely offstage. It teaches the engine to run on its own approval."
