      "text": "protocol that keeps fights small, the plan for the year that actually happens. It is romance expressed as good engineering, and partners who need poetry should watch what the structures are for — them. At work, the date naturally ends up designing how the work works: systems, curricula, pipelines, playbooks. Give it chaos and a deadline; it is quietly happiest there, and colleagues learn to hand over the tangled thing without apologizing for the tangle. Control creeping in The friction: the builder’s hand tightens. What began as helpful structure can become the only acceptable structure, and June 17 can mistake a partner’s improvisation, or a colleague’s mess, for a design flaw needing correction. Not everything wants architecture; some things want air. The"
