      "text": "year. No date in October gets asked its sign more often, and none deserves a table more. In most years since 1940, the Sun’s move from Libra into Scorpio happens during October 23 itself — births before the crossing come out Libra, births after it Scorpio — while a minority of years hand the whole day to Libra and a few give all of it to Scorpio. The table above has your year; the hour does the rest. One day, two owners End-stage Libra and day-one Scorpio share a preoccupation — other people — and disagree about method. The last degrees of Libra read a room to balance it; the first degrees of Scorpio read it to learn what’s underneath. A birth on either side of the line tends to inherit both moves: the Libra Sun’s grace running on the Scorpio Sun’s depth of attention. People describe the result as disarming — friendly on"
